                <airSeal>
We had identified that your house has some excess air leakage and associated heat loss in the following locations:

** The 2nd floor rear wall that adjoins the attic space above the rear main floor addition. Mostly noticed in the bathtub access panel located in the 2nd floor linen closet. A second location was in the rear bedroom closet through some holes in the lower wall inside that closet.

** Up through the heat duct in the main floor near the front entrance. Why there is leakage here is a bit of a mystery, but it was a lot of air coming through, so it will require your further investigation.

** The attic access panel allows leakage around the perimeter and can be sealed with a removeable caulking, such as Draft Attack

** The 6" chimney for the current oil furnace (which will be eliminated if you choose to upgrade the furnace, as you will see further on within this report).

** Both entrance doors and some of the casement windows are not closing tightly. Perhaps they can be better sealed with new weather-stripping or you may choose to upgrade them to new Energy Star rated units during your renovations.

Savings: There should be a minimum 10% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing (especially if the boiler &amp; hot water are upgraded) that could potentially save $ 120 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>
We had identified that your house is reasonably air tight and does rely on those 2 x high wall passive air vents in the upper wall of the hallway outside the bedrooms. As the house requires fresh air (primarily when the kitchen range hood fan, a bathroom fan or the clothes dryer is on), outside fresh air is brought in through those 2 vents

Although your house is reasonably air tight, there are a few upgrades that will help to reduce excess air leakage &amp; heat loss:

** The current co-vented chimney &amp; 6" combustion air supply pipe will be eliminated when you install a new high efficiency combi boiler.

** The kitchen range hood fan was not working while I was there, but we did notice (during the blower door test) there was alot of air leakage coming from the wall that it is installed into (so I expect it may not be well installed &amp; sealed)

** The attic access panel allow leakage around the perimeter and can be sealed with a removeable caulking, such as Draft Attack.

** The bathroom fans appear to be worn and allow air leakage. If you choose to replace them with new ENERGY STARÂ® rated units, these will have better spring loaded dampers inside that help prevent excess heat loss.

Savings: There should be a minimum 10% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing (especially if the boiler &amp; hot water are upgraded) that could potentially save $ 294 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>
Some of the windows in the house were not sealing well and allowing air leakage. I mentioned that I would model the estimated energy &amp; cost savings associated with upgrading them all (including the 3 in the basement) to ENERGY STARÂ® full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K. For your house, I modeled a more efficient window with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/mÂ²K.

Savings:
By replacing all of the current windows in your house with ones that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 207 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 4,225 in rebates (13 windows @ $325 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage</windows>
                <doors>
If you do proceed to replace some windows, you may also consider upgrading one or both of the entrance doors to new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing these 2 entry doors with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 23 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 184 litre electric hot water tank. I recognize that hydro-electricity in Fort Smith is a clean fuel source, however I'm also aware that electricity in Fort Smith has become alot more expensive. As you already have an oil furnace in the house, perhaps you may have an interest in converting the electric hot water tank to a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher (eg: Toyotomi OM-122DW).

This equipment is on display at the Thebacha Campus in Fort Smith and all the new oil tech trades are being trained on how to install &amp; set them up. At the Arctic Energy Alliance, we frequently see them installed in Communities that are not served by hydro electricity, however we are seeing them more in hydro communities as well, as homeowners look for alternatives based on the rising cost of electricity.

Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher, could potentially save $ 472 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an older 82.7% efficient oil furnace, venting up a 6" chimney.

The Thebacha Campus trains the new oil tech trades on the Granby Conforto 95%+ ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This type of furnace is direct-vent (vented out the side wall of the basement, using 2 x PVC pipes) and will no longer require the existing chimney. For your "future planning", I'll model the estimated energy savings of upgrading to this type of furnace.

Savings:
By upgrading to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE (including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat), you could potentially save $ 804 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $3,500 is available for replacing an older oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STARÂ® certified oil fired furnace with a minimum 95% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

PELLET STOVE HEAT FOR THE BASEMENT

With your furnace thermostat on the upper floor, you may find it a bit cool in the basement and perhaps you may consider supplementing with a wood pellet stove &amp; working with your heating contractor to see if you could use your furnace fan to circulate the wood pellet stove throughout the house.

Savings:
For now, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to use a pellet stove to supplement up to 75% of the heating for your basement (as compared to the existing oil furnace). The calculated net savings (reduced oil cost for the furnace, less cost of wood pellets for the pellet stove) is about $239 per year. A qualified wood pellet stove is eligible for 50% of purchase cost up to $ 2,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

Even though the estimated annual savings are not that large, you may still just want to install a pellet stove in the basement to provide supplemental heat to that area, making your time downstairs more enjoyable.
 
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front (South) facing roof, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 4,842 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,225 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 2nd floor bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.4 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented, and can be susceptible to back-drafting of combustion. If you proceed to install a wood pellet stove in the basement, please be sure it has its' own dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe installed and it is directly connected to the back of the stove.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
   - Main floor Range Hood vents inside
   - 2nd Floor Bathroom Fan vents outside
   - Clothes Dryer vents outside

If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort Smith

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:
Electricity - $18 min, $0.25 per kWh
Oil  - $1.35 per litre 
Propane - $0.90 per litre delivered
Wood - $256.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $6.78 per bag, $289.74 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
